{"name":"Elysia Chlorotica","hex":"#9aae07","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[],"entries":[{"title":"Elysia chlorotica","blocks":[{"text":"Elysia chlorotica is a small-to-medium-sized species of green sea slug, a marine opisthobranch gastropod mollusc. This sea slug superficially resembles a nudibranch, yet it does not belong to that clade. Instead it is a member of the clade Sacoglossa, the sap-sucking sea slugs. Some members of this group use chloroplasts from the algae they eat for photosynthesis, a phenomenon known as kleptoplasty. Elysia chlorotica is one species of such \"solar-powered sea slugs\". It lives in a subcellular endosymbiotic relationship with chloroplasts of the marine heterokont alga Vaucheria litorea."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Elysia-chlorotica-body.jpg/330px-Elysia-chlorotica-body.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elysia_chlorotica","wikidata":"Q133568"},{"title":"Elysia (gastropod)","blocks":[{"text":"Elysia is a genus of sea slugs, marine gastropod molluscs in the family Plakobranchidae. These animals are colorful sea slugs, and they can superficially resemble nudibranchs, but are not very closely related to them. Instead they are sacoglossans, commonly known as sap-sucking slugs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Elysia_ornata_003.jpg/330px-Elysia_ornata_003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elysia_(gastropod)","wikidata":"Q1944620"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Elysia-chlorotica-detail.jpg/500px-Elysia-chlorotica-detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Elysia-chlorotica-detail.jpg/500px-Elysia-chlorotica-detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"(A) A defined tubule of the digestive diverticula extending into the parapodial region of the animal (arrow). The digestive system consists of densely packed tubules that branch throughout the animal's body. Each tubule is made up of a layer of single cells containing animal organelles and numerous algal plastids. This cell layer surrounds the lumen. (B) Magnified image of the epidermis of E. chlorotica showing densely packed plastids. The animals are light grey in color without their resident plastids, which contribute chlorophyll to render the sea slugs bright green.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElysia-chlorotica-detail.jpg"}],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"Malagasy green lynx spider (Peucetia madagascariensis) male","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Malagasy_green_lynx_spider_%28Peucetia_madagascariensis%29_male.jpg/500px-Malagasy_green_lynx_spider_%28Peucetia_madagascariensis%29_male.jpg","ratio":1,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malagasy_green_lynx_spider_(Peucetia_madagascariensis)_male.jpg","share":0.635625},{"title":"Lijsterbes (Sorbus). 31-03-2024 (d.j.b.) 02","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Lijsterbes_%28Sorbus%29._31-03-2024_%28d.j.b.%29_02.jpg/500px-Lijsterbes_%28Sorbus%29._31-03-2024_%28d.j.b.%29_02.jpg","ratio":0.7142266335814723,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lijsterbes_(Sorbus)._31-03-2024_(d.j.b.)_02.jpg","share":0.17375},{"title":"Olympic Stadium Munich - Rows of Seats, April 2019 -04","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Olympic_Stadium_Munich_-_Rows_of_Seats%2C_April_2019_-04.jpg/500px-Olympic_Stadium_Munich_-_Rows_of_Seats%2C_April_2019_-04.jpg","ratio":1.5,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olympic_Stadium_Munich_-_Rows_of_Seats,_April_2019_-04.jpg","share":0.15625},{"title":"Pseudatelus sp.","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/